# SMART configuration

The nx-fhir `auth-smart` feature makes this server publish its SMART configuration at `{baseURL}/.well-known/smart-configuration`. A SMART App Launch client reads this document to find the endpoints of the authorization server of `app.auth.issuer`.

The member rules of the document follow SMART App Launch 2.2.0. The feature needs the `auth-resource` and `auth-scopes` features.

## What the feature adds

The feature adds `SmartConfig`, `SmartProperties`, and `SmartConfiguration` under `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/smart/`, and the unit test `SmartConfigurationTest` and the integration test `AuthSmartIT`, which runs under `mvn verify`, under `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/smart/`.

The install writes `app.auth.smart.enabled: false` to `src/test/resources/config/application.yaml` and to `src/test/resources/application-test.yaml`, so a test runs with the feature only when it sets `app.auth.enabled` and `app.auth.smart.enabled` to `true`.

## Configuration

The install writes the configuration below, the `smart` map of `app.auth`. `AUTH-RESOURCE.md` gives the other keys of `app.auth`, and `AUTH-SCOPES.md` gives `app.auth.scopes`.

```yaml
app:
  auth:
    smart:
      enabled: true
      capabilities:
        - launch-standalone
        - client-confidential-symmetric
        - sso-openid-connect
        - permission-offline
        - permission-patient
        - permission-user
        - permission-v1
        - permission-v2
```

- `enabled`: when it and `app.auth.enabled` are both `true`, the server publishes the document. When one of the two is `false` or absent, the feature registers no interceptor, and the server does not publish the document.
- `capabilities`: the document publishes this list as it is written, with no check. The list above is the starter value that the install writes. Write only the capabilities that your authorization server and this server both support.

The capabilities `permission-patient`, `permission-user`, `permission-v1`, and `permission-v2` are true only while `app.auth.scopes.enabled` is `true`, because the scope check of the `auth-scopes` feature enforces them. When you set `app.auth.scopes.enabled` to `false`, remove these four capabilities from `app.auth.smart.capabilities`.

The server reads the OpenID discovery document of the issuer at `{issuer}/.well-known/openid-configuration`. The class `IssuerMetadata` of the `auth-resource` feature reads it at the first request that needs it and keeps it after a successful read, with a connect timeout of 5 seconds and a read timeout of 10 seconds. `app.auth.ssl-verify` of the `auth-resource` feature also applies to this read.

## Where each member comes from

The document copies `token_endpoint` from the issuer document, and each of these members when the issuer document has it: `jwks_uri`, `authorization_endpoint`, `token_endpoint_auth_signing_alg_values_supported`, `scopes_supported`, `response_types_supported`, `introspection_endpoint`, and `revocation_endpoint`. These members differ:

- `issuer`: the `issuer` of the issuer document, only when `capabilities` has `sso-openid-connect`.
- `grant_types_supported`: `authorization_code` of the issuer list, and `client_credentials` only when the issuer lists `private_key_jwt` in `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported`. With no `grant_types_supported` in the issuer document, the value is `["authorization_code"]`.
- `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported`: the entries `client_secret_post`, `client_secret_basic`, and `private_key_jwt` of the issuer list, present when one or more entries are left.
- `code_challenge_methods_supported`: the issuer list with `plain` removed.
- `capabilities`: `app.auth.smart.capabilities`.

Each list that the server takes from the issuer document keeps the order of that document. A member of the issuer document with a wrong JSON type, or a string that is empty or holds only spaces, counts as absent.

## Request

The class `WellKnownInterceptor` of the `auth-resource` feature answers `GET {baseURL}/.well-known/smart-configuration` with the document as one JSON object, status 200, and `Content-Type: application/json`, for each `Accept` header. The request needs no token, because the `auth-resource` feature lets each request under `/fhir/.well-known/` pass. A request with a different method goes on to HAPI FHIR.

The response has status 503 in each of these cases, and the log has one ERROR line with the reason:

- The read of the issuer document fails. The next request reads it again.
- The issuer document has no `token_endpoint`.
- `code_challenge_methods_supported` of the issuer document has no `S256`.
- `capabilities` has `launch-ehr` or `launch-standalone`, and the issuer document has no `authorization_endpoint`.
- `capabilities` has `sso-openid-connect`, and the issuer document has no `issuer` or no `jwks_uri`.
